{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n`.rebar/enrich-drain.lock` is an `O_EXCL` existence lock with no owner stamp and no\nstaleness path. `enrich_drain._acquire_advisory_lock` creates it and never writes to the\nfd; `_release_advisory_lock` unlinks it. Any drainer that dies between those two calls\nleaks a 0-byte file PERMANENTLY: every later drain sees `FileExistsError`, returns\n`None`, and `drain()` logs at INFO and returns `{\"skipped\": \"lock-held\"}`. Enrichment\nthen stops happening with no error and no visible signal. The new `rebar doctor`\nlock-health report (task 513e-35e1-2c80-4428) found exactly this on this host's live\ntracker: a 0-byte drain lock held ~26 days with no drain process alive, so overlap\nenrichment has been silently skipped on that checkout for roughly a month.\n\n## Approach\nGive the drain lock the ownership discipline the store's mkdir write lock already has,\nby REUSING that machinery rather than writing a third locking dialect.\n\n1. **Stamp on acquire.** After the `O_EXCL` create succeeds, write\n   `lock_owner._owner_stamp()` (the existing v2 `host=/ns=/pid=/start=` line) into the\n   file. Best-effort, exactly like `lock.py`'s mkdir stamp write: a failed stamp leaves\n   an unstamped lock, which the wall-clock ceiling still bounds.\n2. **Adjudicate on collision.** Extract the decision table already inside\n   `lock_owner._mkdir_lock_is_stale` into a shared core\n   `_stamp_is_stale(stamp, artifact_path, *, fcntl_held)` that takes an already-read\n   stamp plus the path whose mtime measures the hold age. `_mkdir_lock_is_stale` becomes\n   a thin reader that delegates, so its behaviour is unchanged byte-for-byte (its\n   OSError branch still goes straight to the ceiling, and an empty stamp still reaches\n   `_legacy_stamp_is_stale`). A new sibling `stamped_file_is_stale(path)` reads the\n   stamp from the FILE and delegates to the same core with `fcntl_held=False`. No new\n   threshold, no new pid probe, no second heuristic: the pid-recycle qualification, the\n   refuse-without-proof branches and the 1h `_MKDIR_LOCK_STALE_CEILING_S` backstop are\n   inherited as-is.\n   An EMPTY drain-lock file maps to the core's \"no stamp\" case (ceiling applies), not to\n   the legacy `<host>:<pid>` case: this lock never had a legacy stamp dialect, and an\n   empty file is precisely the pre-fix orphan shape that must become reclaimable.\n3. **Bounded, loud reclaim.** On `FileExistsError`, ask `stamped_file_is_stale`. If it\n   refuses, skip as today. If it says stale, log at WARNING naming the holder stamp and\n   the age (mirroring `_reclaim_mkdir_lock`'s disclosure), unlink, and retry the create\n   EXACTLY ONCE. A second `FileExistsError` means another drainer won the race, so we\n   skip — no loop, no unbounded retry. A lock held by a live, corroborated holder is\n   never broken, because the shared core refuses it.\n4. **Make the skip attributable.** `drain()`'s skip log moves from INFO to WARNING and\n   names the holder decoded from the stamp, so a wedge is visible without running doctor.\n\nWhy this shape: the drain lock is advisory only (it stops two drain PROCESSES from\nredundant work; the queue claim is separately optimistic), so a lost reclaim race costs\nat worst one redundant drain, never correctness. That is why a single bounded retry is\nsufficient and no cross-process arbitration is added.\n\n**Dependency.** `rebar doctor`'s drain leg currently reports `not-assessable` because\nthe lock had no stamp. That row lives in `_commands/doctor_locks.py`, introduced by\ntask 513e-35e1-2c80-4428 (Gerrit change 1701, `LLM-Review +1`, awaiting `Verified`).\nThis change is based on that commit — rebased onto it once it merges, or pushed as its\nstacked child if it has not — and turns the drain row into a real staleness verdict via\nthe same shared function.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/_store/lock_owner.py` — extract `_stamp_is_stale` core; add\n  `stamped_file_is_stale`; `_mkdir_lock_is_stale` delegates (behaviour preserved).\n- `src/rebar/llm/enrich_drain.py` — stamp on acquire; stale-reclaim with one bounded\n  retry; holder description helper; WARNING skip log.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/doctor_locks.py` — the drain leg decodes the stamp and reports a\n  real staleness verdict instead of `not-assessable`.\n- `src/rebar/schemas/doctor.schema.json`, `src/rebar/_cli/help/doctor.txt`,\n  `docs/cli-reference.md` — only if the drain row's documented vocabulary changes.\n- `tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py`, `tests/unit/test_lock_owner.py` (or the existing\n  lock-owner test module), `tests/scripts/graph/test_doctor_locks.py` — tests below.\n\n## Testing\n- **Dead holder is reclaimed** (the bug): write a drain lock file stamped for a\n  provably-dead pid on this host, then call `drain()`/`_acquire_advisory_lock` and\n  assert it acquires, that the lock file now carries OUR stamp, and that the reclaim\n  logged at WARNING.\n- **Legacy unstamped orphan is reclaimed**: a 0-byte drain lock back-dated past the\n  ceiling is acquired; a 0-byte lock created just now is NOT (the create/stamp window\n  is respected).\n- **Live holder is honoured**: a lock stamped with THIS live process is not broken —\n  `drain()` still returns `{\"skipped\": \"lock-held\"}` and the file is byte-identical\n  afterwards. The existing `test_lock_held_skip` covers this shape and must keep passing.\n- **Foreign host / other namespace**: a stamp naming another host is refused while\n  young and reclaimed only past the ceiling — asserting the inherited\n  refuse-without-proof rule rather than a new one.\n- **Release still works**: acquire then release removes the file, so the happy path is\n  unchanged.\n- **`_mkdir_lock_is_stale` unchanged**: the existing lock-owner suite passes untouched,\n  which is the regression guard on the extraction.\n- **Doctor**: a drain lock stamped for a dead pid yields a `stale-lock` finding and exit\n  1; one stamped for a live pid yields no finding and exit 0; doctor still mutates\n  nothing (the read-only test still passes).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A drainer that dies without releasing no longer wedges enrichment: a test leaves a\n      stamped lock file naming a dead pid and asserts the next `drain()` acquires it and\n      processes work.\n- [ ] A live holder is still honoured: a test with a lock stamped for a live pid asserts\n      `drain()` skips and the lock file is unchanged.\n- [ ] The stamp is the existing v2 shape and the staleness verdict comes only from the\n      shared `lock_owner` decision table — verified by a grep of the diff showing no new\n      age threshold and no pid probe used to decide staleness outside `lock_owner`.\n- [ ] The reclaim is bounded and loud: a test asserts at most one retry and a WARNING log\n      line naming the holder stamp and the hold age.\n- [ ] A skipped drain logs at WARNING naming the holder — asserted with `caplog`.\n- [ ] `rebar doctor` reports the drain leg with a real staleness verdict, and a\n      dead-holder drain lock becomes a `stale-lock` finding with exit 1 — asserted in\n      `test_doctor_locks.py`; doctor still reclaims nothing.\n- [ ] All touched `src/rebar` files stay under the 800-LOC module cap and the full local\n      gate set (check_* gates, generators no-diff, lint, typecheck, CI's pytest) is green."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786596748216677001,"uuid":"7ca8a4df-8fd8-4607-a228-062c93f7564d"}